4 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492821 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186492968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492821 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186493002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492821 #edb/ref 1085 "Catherine prepares and sells perfumes. She has ${(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}$ liters of alcohol to prepare them." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492821 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "c4aa5048-3944-50ff-8045-cac8c43fc623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492821 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186492964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492829 #edb/ref 1071 "Catherine accidentally spilled $0.33$ liters of the mixture that she prepared. How much of the mixture did Catherine have left? Express your answer as a decimal.\n\nCatherine had\n{{field-1}}\nliters of mixture left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fe77a68-3c24-5dd5-9917-3c9654562d32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492829 #edb/ref 1074 "In part 3, we found that the volume of the mixture is $4.08$ liters.\n\nTo determine the amount of the mixture Catherine had left, we need to calculate $4.08-0.33.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Catherine had $3.75$ liters of mixture left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492849 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492849 #edb/ref 1071 "Catherine used $9\\,\\frac{2}{7}$ liters of distilled water out of $10\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ liters of distilled water available. What is the volume of the remaining distilled water? Express your answer as a mixed number.\n\nThe volume of distilled water remaining equals$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492849 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a0555b7-c5c1-5a77-9f84-9a3774853f48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492849 #edb/ref 1074 "Caterine had $(10\\,\\frac{3}{4}-9\\,\\frac{2}{7})$ liters of distilled water left over.\n \nWriting $10\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n10\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{10\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{43}{4}.\n$$\n\nWriting $9\\,\\frac{2}{7}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n9\\,\\frac{2}{7}=\\frac{9\\times 7+2}{7}=\\frac{65}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{43}{4}-\\frac{65}{7}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $28.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{43}{4}$ over a denominator of $28,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{43}{4}=\\frac{43\\times 7}{4\\times 7}=\\frac{301}{28}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{65}{7}$ over a denominator of $28,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{65}{7}=\\frac{65\\times 4}{7\\times 4}=\\frac{260}{28}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{301}{28}-\\frac{260}{28}=\\frac{41}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{28}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,13=1\\,\\frac{13}{28}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of distilled water remaining equals $1\\,\\frac{13}{28}$ liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492875 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492875 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492875 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492875 #edb/ref 1071 "To prepare an orange perfume, Catherine must use $0.2$ liters of orange essence for each liter of alcohol. How much orange essence should Catherine use if she plans to use $0.6$ liters of alcohol?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, Catherine must use\n{{field-1}}\nliters of the essence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492875 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74464983-3f15-5a8b-be45-9dcaca36d791" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492875 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the amount of orange essence that Catherine must mix with $0.6$ liters of alcohol, we must multiply $0.6$ by $0.2.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers.\n\nNotice that the second number has a leading $0$, which we ignore during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $0.6$ and there is $1$ decimal place in $0.2.$\nTherefore, their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $12$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{12}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nHence, $0.6\\times 0.2=0.12.$\n\nWe conclude that Catherine must use $0.12$ liters of the essence with $0.6$ liters of alcohol." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492879 #edb/ref 1071 "Catherine mixes $3.4$ liters of alcohol with $0.68$ liters of orange essence. What is the volume of the mixture?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, the volume of the mixture is\n{{field-1}}\nliters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7703677a-4909-5475-b6ab-0c17fa2c2c9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492879 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the volume of the mixture, we need to calculate the sum $3.4+0.68.$\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $3.4$ and $2$ decimal places in $0.68.$\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3.4=3.40\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the mixture is $4.08$ liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492897 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492897 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492897 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction, Catherine has$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of alcohol." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492897 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b192b645-98ae-5323-9ecd-f711bb8584a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492897 #edb/ref 1074 "The exact amount of alcohol is ${(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} & ={{\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{3}}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\times 8}{3\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{64}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492923 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492923 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492923 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492923 #edb/ref 1071 "Catherine sold $6$ identical bottles of perfume, for which she received a total of $$90.33.$ Estimate the price of each bottle by rounding the amount of money Catherine received to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price for each bottle is $\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3c271f0-3313-54ab-8976-eb2aff616b55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492923 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the price of each of the $6$ perfumes, we need to find an approximation for $90.33÷6.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n90.33\\to 90\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $90$ by $6$ as usual:\n\n$$\n90÷6=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price for each bottle is $$15.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08cdc6ce-8d64-5450-a4a2-8a41d3bb4d69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23ad6636-ff9d-5325-9942-5db53540c600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492939 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2eb574e0-03db-54c3-91f3-09274077816c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492954 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492954 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a7ab8c1-2e94-5c2b-b78b-560575faf049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492954 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492964 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186493002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492964 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83d7fbcf-527d-52b2-a43c-36f98275da93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492964 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492968 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492968 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8eabcb6c-2137-5a52-bf99-5ca946764609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492968 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493002 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea9be387-970f-5077-9d94-65ceab1c5ac0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493002 #edb/